To solve the problem of removing duplicate values from a JavaScript array, you can use a combination of several methods depending on your preference for syntax and readability. Here are three different approaches:

### Method 1: Using `Set`
The `Set` object is a built-in JavaScript data structure that allows you to store unique values. You can convert your array to a set, which automatically removes any duplicates, and then convert it back to an array.

```javascript
var names = ["Mike", "Matt", "Nancy", "Adam", "Jenny", "Nancy", "Carl"];
var uniqueNames = [...new Set(names)];
console.log(uniqueNames); // Output: ["Mike", "Matt", "Nancy", "Adam", "Jenny", "Carl"]
```

### Method 2: Using `filter` and `indexOf`
You can use the `filter` method to iterate through the array and check if each element already exists in the resulting array using `indexOf`. If it doesn't exist, you include it.

```javascript
var names = ["Mike", "Matt", "Nancy", "Adam", "Jenny", "Nancy", "Carl"];
var uniqueNames = names.filter(function(name, index, self) {
    return self.indexOf(name) === index;
});
console.log(uniqueNames); // Output: ["Mike", "Matt", "Nancy", "Adam", "Jenny", "Carl"]
```

### Method 3: Using `reduce`
Another approach is to use the `reduce` method to accumulate a new array without duplicates. This method iterates through each element of the original array and checks if it is already present in the accumulator.

```javascript
var names = ["Mike", "Matt", "Nancy", "Adam", "Jenny", "Nancy", "Carl"];
var uniqueNames = names.reduce(function(acc, name) {
    if (!acc.includes(name)) {
        acc.push(name);
    }
    return acc;
}, []);
console.log(uniqueNames); // Output: ["Mike", "Matt", "Nancy", "Adam", "Jenny", "Carl"]
```

All these methods will produce the same output: `["Mike", "Matt", "Nancy", "Adam", "Jenny", "Carl"]`. Choose the method that best fits your personal style or the specific requirements of your project.

