To concatenate text from multiple rows into a single text string in SQL Server, you can use the `STRING_AGG` function available starting from SQL Server 2017. Here's how you can achieve this:

```sql
SELECT STRING_AGG(name, ', ') AS concatenated_names
FROM your_table_name;
```

This query will return a single row with the concatenated names separated by commas.

If you are using an older version of SQL Server (before 2017), you can use a combination of `FOR XML PATH`, `STUFF`, and subqueries to achieve the same result:

```sql
DECLARE @names NVARCHAR(MAX) = '';

SELECT @names += name + ', '
FROM your_table_name;

-- Remove the trailing comma and space
SET @names = STUFF(@names, LEN(@names), 2, '');

SELECT @names AS concatenated_names;
```

Both methods effectively concatenate the names from the specified table into a single string. Choose the method that best fits your SQL Server version.

To export JavaScript array data to CSV format on the client-side, you can use a combination of JavaScript and HTML/CSS. Below is an example of how you can achieve this using Dojo 1.8:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Export Array to CSV</title>
    <!-- Include Dojo library -->
    <script src="https://ajax.googleapis.com/ajax/libs/dojo/1.8.3/dojo/dojo.js"></script>
    <style>
        table {
            width: 100%;
            border-collapse: collapse;
        }
        th, td {
            border: 1px solid black;
            padding: 8px;
            text-align: left;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <button id="exportButton">Export to CSV</button>
    <table id="csvTable" style="display:none;">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>Name</th>
                <th>City Name</th>
                <!-- Add more columns as needed -->
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <script>
        // Sample data
        var data = [["name1", "city_name1"], ["name2", "city_name2"]];

        // Function to generate CSV string from array
        function generateCsv(data) {
            var csvString = '';
            for (var i = 0; i < data.length; i++) {
                var row = '';
                for (var j = 0; j < data[i].length; j++) {
                    row += '"' + data[i][j] + '"';
                    if (j < data[i].length - 1) {
                        row += ',';
                    }
                }
                if (i < data.length - 1) {
                    row += '\n';
                }
                csvString += row;
            }
            return csvString;
        }

        // Event listener for button click
        document.getElementById('exportButton').addEventListener('click', function() {
            var csvData = generateCsv(data);
            var link = document.createElement('a');
            link.href = 'data:text/csv;charset=utf-8,' + encodeURIComponent(csvData);
            link.download = 'data.csv';
            document.body.appendChild(link);
            link.click();
            document.body.removeChild(link);
        });
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```

### Explanation:
