Title: Can we instantiate an abstract class?.
Body: <p>During one of my interview, I was asked "If we can instantiate an abstract class?"</p>

<p>My reply was "No. we can't". But, interviewer told me "Wrong, we can." </p>

<p>I argued a bit on this. Then he told me to try this myself at home. </p>

<pre><code>abstract class my {
    public void mymethod() {
        System.out.print("Abstract");
    }
}

class poly {
    public static void main(String a[]) {
        my m = new my() {};
        m.mymethod();
    }
}
</code></pre>

<p>Here, I'm creating instance of my class and calling method of abstract class. Can anyone please explain this to me? Was I really wrong during my interview?</p>

answer:
Yes, you were wrong during your interview.

In Java, it is not possible to create an instance of an abstract class directly. An abstract class is a class that cannot be instantiated and is intended to serve as a base class for other classes. Abstract classes may contain both abstract methods (methods without implementation) and concrete methods (methods with implementation).

When you attempt to create an instance of `my` by writing `my m = new my() {};`, you are actually trying to create an object of a subclass of `my`. However, since `my` is an abstract class and does not have any concrete implementations, you cannot instantiate it directly.

The correct way to use an abstract class would be to create a subclass of `my` and then instantiate that subclass. Here's how you could modify your code:

```java
// Define the abstract class
abstract class My {
    public abstract void myMethod();

    // Optionally, you can define some concrete methods
    public void defaultMethod() {
        System.out.println("Default Method");
    }
}

// Create a subclass of My
class Poly extends My {
    @Override
    public void myMethod() {
        System.out.println("Poly Class Implementation");
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Poly p = new Poly();
        p.myMethod();  // Outputs: Poly Class Implementation
        p.defaultMethod();  // Outputs: Default Method
    }
}
```

In this modified code:
- We define an abstract class `My` with an abstract method `mymethod`.
- We create a subclass `Poly` that implements `mymethod`.
- We can now create an instance of `Poly` and call its methods, demonstrating that subclasses of abstract classes can indeed be instantiated.

