Title: Does the &#39;mutable&#39; keyword have any purpose other than allowing a data member to be modified by a const member function?.
Body: <p>A while ago, I came across some code that marked a data member of a class with the <code>mutable</code> keyword.  As far as I can see it simply allows you to modify a member in a <code>const</code>-qualified member method:</p>
<pre><code>class Foo  
{  
private:  
    mutable bool done_;  
public:  
    void doSomething() const { ...; done_ = true; }  
};
</code></pre>
<p>Is this the only use of this keyword, or is there more to it than meets the eye? I have since used this technique in a class, marking a <code>boost::mutex</code> as <code>mutable</code>, allowing <code>const</code> functions to lock it for thread-safety reasons, but, to be honest, it feels like a bit of a hack.</p>

answer:
No.

The `mutable` keyword in C++ has no direct purpose other than to allow a data member to be modified by a const member function. It does not provide any additional functionality beyond what would be achieved using public member functions with the same name and arguments, which could also modify the data member.

Here's the relevant part of the standard C++ documentation regarding the `mutable` keyword:

- **4.5.1 The `mutable` Keyword**: "A mutable member variable may be modified from within a const member function."

This means that a `mutable` member variable can be accessed and modified by any member function (including `const` member functions), including those declared inside nested classes. This feature was introduced to enable certain operations that would otherwise be impossible because they violate the constness of the object.

In your example, the `done_` member variable is marked as `mutable`, allowing the `doSomething` function to set its value even though it is declared as `const`. However, it's important to note that using `mutable` should be done judiciously, as it can make the code harder to understand and maintain.

If you need to modify a member variable from a `const` member function, it's usually better to declare the member as non-const or use a helper function that returns a reference to the member. Here's an alternative approach:

```cpp
class Foo {
private:
    bool done_;
public:
    void doSomething() const {
        // Use a helper function to get a non-const reference to done_
        return setDone(true);
    }

private:
    bool setDone(bool value) {
        done_ = value;
        return done_;
    }
};
```

In this version, `setDone` is a private helper function that takes a boolean parameter and sets the `done_` member variable accordingly. This function can then be called from `doSomething`, which remains `const`.

By following these practices, you can ensure that your code remains clear, maintainable, and adheres to best coding practices.

